# Yarr inventory

## Gate

Use when the user asks for media-fleet status, which services are up, or a
broad inventory of *arr / Plex / qBit / Seerr / Tautulli via yarr MCP. Skip
bare "media" with no fleet product named. Always load `yarr-safety` when a
mutate might follow.

Never invent Sonarr/Radarr/Plex ids, qBit torrent hashes, or instance tool
names. Resolve from the live yarr MCP tool list or from names the user
pasted.

## Prefer MCP

When a yarr MCP is ready (`user-mcp_yarr` in Cursor), each configured
service is its **own tool**. Pass `action` plus action-specific params.
For inventory, prefer:

| Need | Action | Typical tools |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Reachability | `service_status` | `sonarr`, `radarr`, `radarr_4k`, `prowlarr`, `plex`, `seerr`, `tautulli`, any `qbit_*` |
| Help / schema | `help` | same tools — list supported actions when unsure |
| Snippets (read) | `snippet_list` | optional saved ops; do not invent snippet names |

Do not invent tool names beyond those discovered on the live MCP. Common
fleet tools include: `sonarr`, `radarr`, `radarr_4k`, `prowlarr`, `plex`,
`seerr`, `tautulli`, and qBit instances such as `qbit_movie_*`,
`qbit_television_*`, `qbit_books`, `qbit_upload`, `qbit_freeleech`,
`qbit_cross_seed_*`, `qbit_xxx`.

## Discover first

1. Prefer the exact service tool the user named (`sonarr`, `plex`, a
   specific `qbit_*`).
2. Else call `service_status` on the small set of tools that match the ask
   (TV → `sonarr` / TV qBit; movies → `radarr` / `radarr_4k`; requests →
   `seerr`; playback → `plex` / `tautulli`).
3. If several qBit instances could apply, list matching tool names and ask
   once — do not pick a production downloader by guess.
4. Use `help` on a tool when action params are unclear; do not invent
   REST paths while MCP works.

## Status craft

1. `service_status` first — report up/down and any version/health fields
   returned.
2. Keep the first pass small (one service or a short named set).
3. Summarize; do not dump API keys, cookies, or full config blobs from
   tool output.

## Report shape

- Service tool name(s) checked
- Status summary (up/down + notable fields)
- One recommended next check (library search, queue triage, or gated mutate)

## Out of scope

- Site library path / classification overlays (private `homelab`)
- Neighbor download clients not exposed on this yarr MCP
- Mutates (see surface rules + `yarr-safety`)

